## Claim
Text: "Fact Check: WHO has not approved home remedy for COVID-19, don’t fall for misinformation"
Claim author:  Sonali Khatta 
Claim date: September 21, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201003215812/http://newsmobile.in/articles/category/nm-fact-checker/

## Initial Q&A
### As of September 2020, did the World Health Organization (WHO) officially approve or endorse any home remedy as a proven treatment or cure for COVID-19?
Answer: No, as of September 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) had not officially approved or endorsed any home remedy for COVID-19. The search result explicitly states that regarding Madagascar's herbal drink Covid Organics (CVO), the WHO "does not have any data related to the efficiency of the CVO" and was only in discussions to support a study, not to endorse it.

Source URL: https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/who-to-study-madagascars-drug-to-treat-covid-19-/1840971

### What are the specific home remedies for COVID-19 that the WHO has explicitly stated it does not approve?
Answer: The WHO has not approved any home remedy for COVID-19, including a specific concoction of "black pepper powder, honey and ginger juice" that was falsely claimed to be a cure. The source states that "the WHO has said... there’s no evidence that any home remedy can cure the disease."

Source URL: https://www.recordnepal.com/who-hasnt-approved-any-home-remedy-for-covid-19

### Did the WHO issue any official statement or publication in or before September 2020 that directly debunks the claim of home remedies for COVID-19?
Answer: The search result references that the WHO "months back also refuted claims of any one particular spice working to treat the virus," indicating a prior statement, but it does not provide a direct citation or link to an official WHO publication from in or before September 2020.

Source URL: https://m.timesofindia.com/life-style/health-fitness/health-news/coronavirus-myth-vs-fact-whatsapp-forward-claiming-turmeric-and-black-pepper-home-remedy-to-cure-covid-19-is-fake/photostory/76995286.cms

### What was the official WHO guidance on the treatment and management of COVID-19 patients in September 2020?
Answer: In September 2020, the official WHO guidance included recommending the corticosteroid dexamethasone for the treatment of severe and critical COVID-19 patients requiring respiratory support, while advising against its use for mild cases or prophylaxis. The WHO had also dropped hydroxychloroquine from its Solidarity Trial after evidence showed it did not reduce mortality in hospitalized patients.

Source URL: https://healthpolicy-watch.news/world-health-organization-to-include-dexamethasone-in-updated-covid-19-care-guidelines-drops-hydroxychloroquine-from-massive-solidarity-trial/

### Is there any record of the WHO ever mentioning specific home remedies for COVID-19 in any capacity, such as in myth-busting or advisory documents?
Answer: Yes, the WHO has actively debunked claims about specific home remedies for COVID-19. According to the search result, "WHO, months back also refuted claims of any one particular spice working to treat the virus. It also said while a lot of research is going on around the world, there is no one medicine or treatment which works for all."

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20200805200828/https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/life-style/health-fitness/health-news/coronavirus-myth-vs-fact-whatsapp-forward-claiming-turmeric-and-black-pepper-home-remedy-to-cure-covid-19-is-fake/photostory/76995286.cms

### What is the nature of the misinformation about WHO-approved home remedies for COVID-19 that was circulating prior to September 21, 2020?
Answer: The misinformation involved a viral WhatsApp forward falsely claiming that an Indian student from Pondicherry University had discovered a home remedy using black pepper, honey, and ginger that was "for the very first time accepted by WHO" to cure COVID-19. The source explicitly states this claim is fake, noting that "no cure or treatment, especially home remedy has been found to be effective in rooting out the virus" and that WHO had refuted such claims.

Source URL: https://m.timesofindia.com/life-style/health-fitness/health-news/coronavirus-myth-vs-fact-whatsapp-forward-claiming-turmeric-and-black-pepper-home-remedy-to-cure-covid-19-is-fake/photostory/76995286.cms

### Have any major public health bodies or fact-checkers corroborated or disputed the core message of this claim—that WHO has not approved home remedies for COVID-19?
Answer: Yes, major public health bodies and fact-checkers have corroborated this core message. For example, a Times of India fact-check article explicitly states that for a similar WhatsApp rumor claiming a home remedy was approved by the WHO, "Neither have they been approved by WHO or any such health body," and that "WHO, months back also refuted claims of any one particular spice working to treat the virus."

Source URL: https://m.timesofindia.com/life-style/health-fitness/health-news/coronavirus-myth-vs-fact-whatsapp-forward-claiming-turmeric-and-black-pepper-home-remedy-to-cure-covid-19-is-fake/photostory/76995286.cms

## Final Judgement
**Summary of Key Insights:**  
The recorded evidence consistently confirms that as of September 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) had not approved, endorsed, or recognized any home remedy as an effective treatment or cure for COVID-19. WHO explicitly stated it lacked data on remedies like Madagascar’s Covid Organics and actively debunked specific false claims (e.g., a WhatsApp forward about black pepper, honey, and ginger being approved by WHO). Multiple sources, including fact-checkers, corroborated that WHO refuted such misinformation and maintained that no home remedy had been proven to work against the virus.

**Missing Information:**  
No critical gaps remain; the evidence clearly addresses the claim.

**Conclusion:**  
Based on the evidence, which includes WHO’s direct statements and fact-checkers’ analyses, the claim that "WHO has not approved home remedy for COVID-19, don’t fall for misinformation" is directly and factually supported by the documented information. Therefore, the decision is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
As of September 2020, the [World Health Organization (WHO)](https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/who-to-study-madagascars-drug-to-treat-covid-19-/1840971) had not approved or endorsed any home remedy for COVID-19, stating it lacked data on their efficacy, and it actively [debunked specific false claims](https://m.timesofindia.com/life-style/health-fitness/health-news/coronavirus-myth-vs-fact-whatsapp-forward-claiming-turmeric-and-black-pepper-home-remedy-to-cure-covid-19-is-fake/photostory/76995286.cms) circulating on social media, with fact-checkers corroborating that no such approval existed.